WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:01.799
Speaker 1: You know, the last week or so,
I've been thinking to some degree.

00:00:01.799 --> 00:00:02.799
It seems as though so-called wokeness is actually
the prevailing opinion.

00:00:02.799 --> 00:00:03.799
In a way, it seems as though Wokeness is winning
in the United States.

00:00:03.799 --> 00:00:04.799
And Eugene Robinson from The Washington Post
wrote a really great piece yesterday called

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:05.799
Wokeness is Winning, and it makes some excellent
points backed by facts, which I'm going to

00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:06.799
get to in a moment.

00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:08.130
But when I started to suspect that this entire
anti-woke framing and campaign and obsession

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:11.019
from the right, maybe a bit of an overplay
of the hand that they are that they are currently

00:00:11.019 --> 00:00:12.480
playing was polling out of Florida that a
lot of Ron Desantis's extreme anti-woke stuff

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.139
could potentially backfire because by and
large a majority of the country seems to sympathize

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:15.139
with the woke perspectives, even when defined
as woke.

00:00:15.139 --> 00:00:16.139
Now there's two different things here.

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:17.139
Okay, let me give you an analogy.

00:00:17.139 --> 00:00:18.940
When we talk about health care, you could
say the government has a duty to provide a

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.950
basic level of care to everybody, regardless
of ability to pay very high support for that

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:21.950
claim.

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:23.250
On the other hand, if you say there should
be a government takeover of your health care,

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:24.660
very low support for that claim.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.730
But when we actually dissect what do we mean
here and accurately represent what things

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:33.910
are, we see that people generally tend to
agree more than they disagree with the left

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:35.340
wing perspective on health care.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:40.490
The government does have a basic duty to provide
an entry level of care at minimum to everybody.

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:42.690
If you say to people, are you in favor of
wokeness versus if you say, do you think that

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:47.559
it's important to promote equality of opportunity
and accept people who are trans and businesses

00:00:47.559 --> 00:00:49.449
should take steps to promote diversity?

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:53.739
When you ask people about those individual
issues, there is significant support for them.

00:00:53.739 --> 00:00:57.070
But then you go, Well, what about woke?

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:58.320
Are you woke?

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.399
Much lower level of support.

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:05.549
Case in point in Eugene Robinson's piece in
The Washington Post, he talks about a survey

00:01:05.549 --> 00:01:06.659
that was conducted by the Nonpartizan Reach
Research Institute, NORC, at the University

00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:07.659
of Chicago.

00:01:07.659 --> 00:01:12.770
They do a lot of different opinion polling,
and we often cite them on the show.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:16.080
They looked at actual so-called woke issues
and found that the woke side of the equation

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.610
is way more popular than you might think based
on what you see on Fox News or elsewhere on

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:24.450
the issue of, quote, accepting people who
are transgender. 56% of respondents said either

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:29.250
society has been about right or hasn't gone
far enough when it comes to accepting people

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:30.250
who are trans.

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:31.439
The woke positions are a majority.

00:01:31.439 --> 00:01:34.229
The minority thinks that acceptance of trans
people has gone too far.

00:01:34.229 --> 00:01:37.250
Another example on the issue of promoting
equality between men and women.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:42.229
86% of respondents say that society is either
where it should be or hasn't gone far enough

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:44.610
to promote equality between men and women.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:47.020
That's a woke country by their term now, of
course.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:50.100
Woke as a pejorative is used to criticize
these positions.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.880
But the point here is when you ask people
about their views on issues, they very much

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.740
are in favor on, quote, accepting people who
are gay, lesbian or bisexual.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.860
The poll found that 69% of respondents believe
that society has been well calibrated on promoting

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:02.860
acceptance or has not gone far enough.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:03.860
That is very woke.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.560
And then lastly, on businesses taking steps
to promote racial and ethnic diversity.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:08.759
Woke beats Anti-woke 70 to 28%.

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:14.110
Even when you ask about schools and universities
taking steps to promote racial and ethnic

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.090
diversity, again, Wokeness wins 67 to 30.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:20.620
Last aspect to this specifically about schools
in the context of banning drag shows and banning

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.030
books and all of it.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.400
When respondents were asked which of these
concerns you more about schools today, given

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.970
the choice of two statements, are you more
concerned about schools banning books and

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:29.280
censoring topics of educational importance,
or are you more concerned about are you in

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:30.280
favor of schools doing that and concerned
that inappropriate things may be taught to

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.510
your kids By an almost 2 to 1 margin, people
are more.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:35.860
Concerned about the banning of books and the
limiting of teaching in schools rather than,

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.250
oh, my kid might be taught something inappropriate.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.190
So there's a couple of important takeaways
from this.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:43.060
Number one, as another reminder, Reddit and
Twitter aren't real life in the sense that

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.730
ideas can be over or underrepresented in those
sphere spheres.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:50.930
And when you actually talk to people just
out there, you get dramatically different

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:51.930
results.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:52.930
And number two, even the concerted effort
of how has it been a year?

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:53.930
Has it been two years, three years?

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:54.970
It's kind of all the time where but certainly
we've seen 1 to 3 years of a concerted effort

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:55.970
to go after a lot of these same elements.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:56.970
Despite all of that, it hasn't been that effective
at actually convincing people to take so-called

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:57.970
anti-woke positions, which I would call, in
many cases, just anti-science positions.

00:02:57.970 --> 00:02:58.970
And so for someone like a Ron DeSantis, who's
about Florida is where woke comes to die and

00:02:58.970 --> 00:02:59.970
on and on and on, it may help him in the Republican
primary in some states, not necessarily even

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:00.970
all, but it may not be super useful nationally
if he were to win the nomination.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:05.140
Ron DeSantis that is in running against the
eventual Democratic nominee, because it still

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.020
seems that even after a blitz of 1 to 3 years
just attacking these views and these positions,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.190
most Americans actually come down on what
is often characterized as the woke position

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:11.670
will be an interesting thing to see in 24
when it comes to strategy.

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:14.120
I meant to play this earlier in the week and
I wasn't able to get to it and I don't want

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:15.120
to skip over it because it is so strange and
interesting.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:16.760
Donald Trump put out a statement about how
he helped the farmers avoid an estate tax,

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:17.760
but in the discussion of it, he talks about
how if you don't love your kids, you don't

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:18.760
have to leave them your farm.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:19.760
And it has a very weird undertone where many
people saw this and speculated and wrote to

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:20.760
me.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.409
Is this Trump signaling he doesn't love his
kids and he's not leaving them his an inheritance?

00:03:22.409 --> 00:03:23.409
It is very strange.

00:03:23.409 --> 00:03:25.190
I want you to take a look at this and tell
me what you think.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:27.622
Speaker 3: And I made farmers happy and rich
again, and they're doing a fantastic job.

00:03:27.622 --> 00:03:28.622
And you know what?

00:03:28.622 --> 00:03:31.159
Someday it'll become time for them to leave
this beautiful earth, and they'll be able

00:03:31.159 --> 00:03:32.269
to leave their farm without taxes.

00:03:32.269 --> 00:03:33.269
All right.

00:03:33.269 --> 00:03:34.269
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:03:34.269 --> 00:03:35.269
So far, I understand the policy to their children.

00:03:35.269 --> 00:03:36.590
Speaker 3: I got rid of the death tax on farms
so that when you do pass away on the assumption

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:37.590
that you love your children, you can leave
it to them and they won't have to pay tax.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:38.590
But if you don't love your children so much
and there are some people that don't, and

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:39.590
maybe deservedly so, it won't matter because
frankly, you don't have to leave them anything.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:40.590
Thank you very much.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:41.590
Have fun.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:42.590
Speaker 1: Have fun with that, guys, huh?

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:43.590
Our friend Aaron Roper on Twitter said that
he had to watch this two times merely to convince

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:44.590
himself that it wasn't some kind of deepfake
AI prank because it's so bizarre.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:45.590
So a couple of different things here.

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:46.590
First of all, it sort of seems like this is
being written into Trump Scripts because he

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:47.590
said something very similar on March 13th
in Iowa, and he said it in an almost identical

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:48.590
way.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:49.590
And then, of course, the bigger question that
many of you are wondering about is, is this

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:50.590
Donald Trump signaling something to his own
children?

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:51.590
Is this Donald Trump signaling that that maybe
it is he who doesn't love his kids?

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:52.590
It's absolutely bizarre and strange.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:53.590
And the focus now multiple times on people
who don't love their kids.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:54.590
Really strange stuff.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:55.590
I don't know what to make of it.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:55.599
Let me know in the comments what you think

